# insp-v1 — static code-inspection policy for worked example S9
#
# Exercises the insp@1 certificate path end-to-end (issue #260), and shows the
# layering the backend needs to be useful. Three rules:
#
#   * absence_recheck — STRICT. The pure negative existential: given an
#     exhaustive inspection of a source unit, conclude that a feature occurs
#     nowhere in it. This is the closed-world step, and it is the only reason
#     a code inspection is a strict step at all rather than a measurement
#     (lean/Lara/Insp.lean, relHolds_absent_of_nil).
#
#   * plan_vs_shipped_recheck — STRICT. The corpus's own code-inspection
#     scheme (M0 C13: `plan_vs_shipped_diff`, 3 of 60 sampled claims): the
#     feature is enumerated in the plan's inspection and absent from the
#     shipped module's. Two inventories, one certificate.
#
#   * implementation_gap_claim — DEFEASIBLE. The bridge. It consumes the
#     strict diff together with a version-match attestation and concludes the
#     scientific claim.
#
# The split is the whole point, and here it is sharper than for ord@1. What
# the certificate settles is the inference from a DECLARED EXHAUSTIVE
# ENUMERATION to a structural conclusion. It settles nothing about whether the
# enumeration is faithful to the bytes — that the inspection really covered
# the whole module, and that it read the version of the source the claim is
# about. Byte-level evidence admission is not part of v0.1 (spec §4.3), so
# both of those stay defeasible.
#
# The corpus unit this example is drawn from
# (corpus-units/rebench-rust_codecontests/C09) is exactly the case where the
# coverage half is met and the version half is NOT: notes.md, the sole
# documentary basis for the "planned" half, ships nowhere in the artifact
# tree. Its honest verdict is a `gap`, and no certificate changes that. S9
# supplies the version attestation that C09 could not, so the same shape
# reaches `justified` — which is what makes the missing half visible as the
# thing it is.

policy insp-v1

# --- Signature (spec §2, §3.4; lara-core@0.2, issue #89) -------------------------------------------
# The many-sorted proposition signature the checker enforces as rejection class R2 at stage 2:
# every predicate and constructor symbol this policy's rules and programs may use, with the sort
# of each argument position. `Num` and `Str` are the two built-in base sorts (the sorts of numeric
# and string literals) and are not declarable.
#
# Generated by `scripts/infer-sigma.hs` (union-find over argument occurrences, finest consistent
# partition), then the sorts were given their intended names — the one editorial step, and the
# only one:
#
#   cabal exec -- runghc --ghc-arg=-package --ghc-arg=lara scripts/infer-sigma.hs \
#     examples/S9/insp-v1.policy.lara examples/S9/example.lara

sort Unit, Feature, Findings, Inspection

# The inspected source units: a planning document and a shipped module. Both
# are `Unit` because both are inspected the same way — the asymmetry is in the
# rule that reads them, not in the things themselves.
con plan_notes : Unit
con solver_module : Unit

# The features an inspection enumerates. Each is a nullary constant: what the
# inspection found, named.
con generation_call : Feature
con python_translate : Feature
con repair_pass : Feature
con vote_call : Feature

# The three RESERVED inventory constructors (src/Lara/Strict/Insp.hs `Con`;
# lean/Lara/Insp.lean `Con`). `inv(U, Fs)` is an exhaustive inspection of `U`
# whose findings are `Fs`; `finding`/`no_findings` are the cons spine that
# carries them. The spine exists because Σ fixes each constructor's arity
# (spec §2) — a variadic `inv(U, f1, …, fn)` could not be declared here at all,
# and this is the reason the backend's convention is a list rather than a
# variadic node.
con inv(Unit, Findings) : Inspection
con finding(Feature, Findings) : Findings
con no_findings : Findings

# `inspected_for(U, F, I)` — an exhaustive inspection of `U`, looking for `F`,
# whose result is `I`. The feature rides on the premise rather than only on the
# conclusion because every rule parameter must be bound by matching a premise
# (`inferTheta`'s `ThetaParameterUnbound`); it also reads correctly, since an
# inspection is always an inspection FOR something.
pred inspected_for(Unit, Feature, Inspection)

pred code_absent(Unit, Feature)
pred code_planned_not_shipped(Unit, Unit, Feature)
pred implementation_gap(Unit, Feature)
pred version_matched(Unit)

# --- The strict legs: certificate-checked structural facts -----------------------------------
# Slot order is the premise list order, and the certificate cites it. The
# one-inventory certificate `(inspect (prem 0))` and the two-inventory
# `(inspectdiff (prem 0) (prem 1))` are separate wire keywords with separate
# flat schemas, because arity is part of the family: an `(inspect …)` payload
# under a diff goal is a rejection, not a re-interpretation.
#
# Every consulted inventory traces to a premise slot. insp@1 rejects a slot
# naming a theory entry outright, which matters more here than in either
# arithmetic sibling: the value the guard protects is the closed-world premise
# itself, and a self-supplied theory entry asserting "I inspected everything
# and found nothing" would never be seen by a leaf or admission check.

rule absence_recheck(S, F, I)
  mode       = strict
  premises   = [ inspected_for(S, F, I) ]
  conclusion = code_absent(S, F)
  allow-trusted = false
  certifiers = [ (insp@1, sha256:insp-v1-theory-0) ]

rule plan_vs_shipped_recheck(P, S, F, IP, IS)
  mode       = strict
  premises   = [ plan:    inspected_for(P, F, IP),
                 shipped: inspected_for(S, F, IS) ]
  conclusion = code_planned_not_shipped(P, S, F)
  allow-trusted = false
  certifiers = [ (insp@1, sha256:insp-v1-theory-0) ]

# The empty theory the insp@1 certificates cite: the certified step is the
# closed-world inference over premise-supplied enumerations, so the theory
# contributes no entries — the digest is selection identity only (the S1
# precedent). Emptiness here is a property of THIS policy, which the .lara door
# pins; what insp@1 enforces is that theory entries are UNCITABLE.
theory sha256:insp-v1-theory-0 = []

# --- The bridge: from a structural fact to a scientific claim --------------------------------
# The strict conclusion says the plan enumerated the feature and the shipped
# module did not. It does not say the plan document is the one this artifact
# shipped against. That identity is the corpus's `version_match` critical
# question, and it enters as attested evidence like any other.

rule implementation_gap_claim(P, S, F)
  mode       = defeasible
  premises   = [ diff:    code_planned_not_shipped(P, S, F),
                 version: version_matched(P) ]
  conclusion = implementation_gap(S, F)

# WELL-FORMEDNESS (spec §8.1, Path B): no contrary pair is declared, so no
# contrary side is strict-reachable and the policy passes the R12 check.
#
# That is a deliberate consequence of where the conflict lives, and it differs
# from the ord@1 story. `num_lt`/`num_le` declare no contrary pair because they
# CANNOT conflict: comparison goals are decided against a shared ground truth,
# so a sound backend never accepts two conflicting members
# (lean/Lara/Ord.lean, ordModels_excl_of_lt). Inspection goals are decided
# against a DECLARED inventory, and two units may declare different
# inventories for the same source — `code_absent` and `code_present` over one
# source and feature are genuinely co-acceptable
# (lean/Lara/Insp.lean, inspModels_absent_present_sat).
#
# So the conflict is real, and Path B says where it may be carried: a strict
# conclusion may not overlap either side of a `contrary` declaration. A policy
# that made `code_absent`/`code_present` strict conclusions AND declared them
# contrary would be an R12 rejection. The conflict therefore belongs on the
# DEFEASIBLE bridge's conclusions — `implementation_gap` against whatever a
# policy declares its contradictory — which is exactly the layering this file
# uses. S9 has no rival argument, so it declares no pair; a policy that admits
# one must declare it there, not on the raw inspection atoms.
